      "name": "InitializeKeyboardVirtualKeyCodes",
      "signature": "void InitializeKeyboardVirtualKeyCodes(void)",
      "calling_convention": "__stdcall",
      "comment": "Initializes keyboard virtual key code mappings for all standard keyboard keys.\\n\\nALGORITHM:\\n1. Sets up C++ exception handling frame with exception context saved to stack\\n2. Initializes keyboard mapping entry 0 via setup_location_entry()\\n3. Iterates through keyboard key indices 1-224+ setting keyboard index counter\\n4. For each keyboard index:\\n   - Stores index byte in keyboardIndexCounter stack variable [EBP-0x4]\\n   - Loads address of keyboard mapping data structure in ECX\\n   - Pushes virtual key code argument on stack\\n   - Calls setup_location_entry() to register the key mapping\\n   - Optionally writes numeric VK code to data location (0x08, 0x09, 0x0D, etc.)\\n5. After all keyboard mappings initialized, calls FUN_10233b13() for post-initialization\\n6. Restores exception handler context and returns with RET (stdcall cleanup)\\n\\nPARAMETERS:\\nNone - Function takes no parameters, initializes global keyboard mapping tables\\n\\nRETURNS:\\nvoid - No return value, function modifies global data structures\\n\\nSPECIAL CASES:\\n- Exception frame setup: local_4 = -1 initially, then set to 0 after first call\\n- Duplicate key indices: indices 99/100 and 131/136 have identical values\\n- Virtual key codes match Windows API constants (VK_BACK=0x08, VK_RETURN=0x0D, etc.)\\n- Function keys F1-F12 use virtual codes 0x70-0x7B\\n- Numpad keys use virtual codes 0x60-0x6F\\n- Left/Right modifier variants (Shift, Ctrl, Alt) have separate entries\\n- Post-initialization function receives 0x10247620 as argument (game-specific context)",
      "name_source": "LoD/PD2",
      "method": "STR",
      "index": "STR:72236d861f9f82bc4a174082adac9200",
